1
00:00:00,340 --> 00:00:03,403
We will compare models using a synthetic
issue.

2
00:00:03,583 --> 00:00:08,639
The order validator accepts zero even
though an order needs at least one item.

3
00:00:08,819 --> 00:00:10,305
Our target is clear.

4
00:00:10,485 --> 00:00:15,117
Find the cause, suggest a small change,
and propose a regression test while

5
00:00:15,117 --> 00:00:17,217
preserving the function signature.

6
00:00:17,397 --> 00:00:22,701
A task with an observable outcome makes
the comparison easier to evaluate than an

7
00:00:22,701 --> 00:00:24,011
open ended question.

8
00:00:25,465 --> 00:00:30,074
In VS Code, open Copilot Chat and look at
the model selector.

9
00:00:30,254 --> 00:00:33,498
Pick two workplace approved options if you
have them.

10
00:00:33,678 --> 00:00:38,308
One might favor speed and the other may be
oriented toward harder reasoning.

11
00:00:38,488 --> 00:00:43,998
If your account exposes only one model,
compare two runs with that model and label

12
00:00:43,998 --> 00:00:45,678
the exercise accordingly.

13
00:00:45,858 --> 00:00:49,282
The important part is recording your
starting conditions.

14
00:00:50,757 --> 00:00:56,225
We attach the issue, source, and tests in
each new chat, then give each option the

15
00:00:56,225 --> 00:00:57,092
same request.

16
00:00:57,272 --> 00:01:01,671
Keep the task in an explanation or
planning mode to avoid changing the

17
00:01:01,671 --> 00:01:03,619
working tree during comparison.

18
00:01:03,799 --> 00:01:09,045
Changing the attached files, agent mode,
or requirements would change the task.

19
00:01:09,225 --> 00:01:13,546
Keep those inputs stable and record any
differences you cannot avoid.

20
00:01:15,007 --> 00:01:19,410
Look for the line responsible for
accepting zero and check whether the

21
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:22,303
proposed test would fail on the original
code.

22
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:27,465
Note how long each response takes and
whether a response adds unnecessary work.

23
00:01:27,645 --> 00:01:29,993
The results on screen are illustrative.

24
00:01:30,173 --> 00:01:34,355
Try the exercise yourself and record the
outcome you actually saw.

25
00:01:34,535 --> 00:01:40,006
One run gives a useful observation, with
limited evidence for broader conclusions.

26
00:01:41,465 --> 00:01:46,550
For this small boundary bug, a fast option
may be enough once tests confirm the

27
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:47,001
result.

28
00:01:47,181 --> 00:01:51,807
When a change spans packages, has
ambiguous requirements, or requires

29
00:01:51,807 --> 00:01:56,769
comparing several designs, you might
evaluate a stronger reasoning option.

30
00:01:56,949 --> 00:02:01,107
A broader change still needs clear context
and independent checks.

31
00:02:01,287 --> 00:02:04,082
The model label does not replace either
one.

32
00:02:05,548 --> 00:02:09,056
One answer suggests testing one, which
already passes.

33
00:02:09,236 --> 00:02:13,633
Another adds the test for zero, which
fails against the original code.

34
00:02:13,813 --> 00:02:17,951
The second test directly exercises the
behavior in the issue.

35
00:02:18,131 --> 00:02:23,122
To compare responses, inspect those
details and run the relevant checks in the

36
00:02:23,122 --> 00:02:25,042
approved training environment.

37
00:02:25,222 --> 00:02:29,938
This tells you more than whether the
generated explanation sounded confident.

38
00:02:31,382 --> 00:02:34,890
Run the benchmark and write one sentence
about your choice.

39
00:02:35,070 --> 00:02:39,993
Include which model you used, what it got
right or missed, and the test you would

40
00:02:39,993 --> 00:02:41,938
use to verify a proposed change.

41
00:02:42,118 --> 00:02:47,240
If both results fall short, adjust the
brief or fix the missing context before

42
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:48,422
increasing effort.

43
00:02:48,602 --> 00:02:53,390
Keep the comparison small enough that you
can redo it when the catalog changes.
